<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St Vitus Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1344">1344</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as St. Vitus Cathedral</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St Vitus Cathedral is the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-castle">Prague Castle</a>. Begun in 1344 under Charles IV by Matthias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> and continued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-parler">Peter Parler</a>, it was left unfinished during the Hussite Wars and completed only in 1929. It holds the tombs of Bohemian kings and saints, including St Wenceslas, and the crown jewels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> are kept there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral.jpg" alt="St Vitus Cathedral" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MathKnight and Zachi Evenor · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PragueCathedral03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The present cathedral is the third church on its site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-castle">Prague Castle</a>, all dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-vitus">Saint Vitus</a>. The first was an early Romanesque rotunda founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-i-duke-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus I, Duke of Bohemia</a>, in 930, after he obtained a relic of the saint's arm from Emperor Henry I. In 1060 Prince Spytihněv II began a larger Romanesque basilica, which incorporated the rotunda's apse housing the tomb of Saint Wenceslaus. The present Gothic building was begun on 21 November 1344, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> was raised to an archbishopric and King John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> laid the foundation stone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv-duke-of-anjou">Charles IV</a> intended it as a coronation church, a family crypt and a treasury of relics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The first master builder was Matthias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>, a Frenchman summoned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, who planned a French Gothic basilica but lived to build only the easternmost parts of the choir. After his death in 1352 the 23-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-parler">Peter Parler</a> took over and developed his own style, with net vaults, varied window tracery and sculpted busts of the royal family, saints and the two master builders. At Parler's death only the choir and parts of the transept were finished. His sons continued, completing the south tower and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-gate-bhaktapur">Golden Gate</a>, through which kings entered for coronations.</p><p>The cathedral is 124 metres long and 60 metres wide, and its main tower is 102.8 metres high. The Chapel of Saint Wenceslaus, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-parler">Peter Parler</a> between 1356 and 1364, has walls decorated with over 1,300 semi-precious stones, and a small door with seven locks leads to the chamber holding the Bohemian crown jewels. Since 1997 the cathedral has been dedicated to Saints Vitus, Wenceslaus and Adalbert.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Hussite War halted construction, and Hussite iconoclasm damaged the furnishings. A fire in 1541 caused heavy damage. For centuries the cathedral stood half-finished, and a baroque spire was added to the south tower between 1753 and 1775. A society formed in 1844 to complete it, and work began on a new nave in 1870 under Josef Mocker, who designed the west facade, with Kamil Hilbert completing it. Alfons Mucha designed windows in the nave, and by the jubilee of Saint Wenceslaus in 1929 the cathedral was finished, nearly 600 years after it was begun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St. Vitus Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The university he founded in 1348, today Charles University, was the oldest in Central Europe, and the Gothic architecture of Peter Parler at <b>St Vitus Cathedral</b> and on the Charles Bridge, together with the castle of Karlštejn, shaped the art of the whole region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
